**Coeliac disease** is an **autoimmune condition**. When people with coeliac disease eat **gluten**, a protein found in **wheat, barley and rye**, their immune system attacks the lining of the small intestine. This stops the body absorbing nutrients properly. Around **1 in 100 people** in the UK have it, but most have not been diagnosed.

## Symptoms

- **diarrhoea**, or constipation
- **tummy pain**, bloating and wind
- feeling very **tired**, often because of **anaemia**
- losing weight without trying
- an itchy, blistering rash (dermatitis herpetiformis)
- mouth ulcers
- in children, not growing as expected

Some people have mild symptoms or none at all.

## Who should be tested?

You should be offered a test if you have symptoms, or conditions linked to coeliac disease such as **type 1 diabetes**, **autoimmune thyroid disease**, **IBS**, or a close relative with coeliac disease.

## Getting diagnosed

A **blood test** looks for antibodies. It only works if you are **eating gluten**. Keep eating gluten, in more than one meal every day for at least 6 weeks, until testing is finished. If the blood test is positive, you may be referred for an **endoscopy** and **biopsy** to confirm it.

Read about [coeliac disease treatment](/conditions/digestive-and-liver/coeliac-disease/treatment/).
